{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 860 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "98ef7cff3f3ea65286bd8daa78c1b22fc1456c7271e1a21eca5fe942822e4e98" :edb/db-before-t 859 :edb/db-after-t 860 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534172 50 #inst "2026-10-06T07:22:10.761Z" 13194139534172 true] [17592186128844 1145 "Our function is a radical function, so it is continuous over all points in its domain.\n\nTo find the domain of $\\sqrt{4-x}$, we set the expression under the radical to be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n4-x\\geq 0\n\\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad \nx\\leq 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $f(x)$ is $(-\\infty,4]$, and the function is continuous on this interval." 13194139534172 true] [17592186128844 1145 "Our function is a radical function, so it is continuous over all points in its domain.\n\nTo find the domain of $\\sqrt{4-x}$, we set the expression under the radical to be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n4-x\\geq 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow\\qquad x\\leq 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $f(x)$ is $[MATH: (-\\infty,4]]$, and the function is continuous on this interval." 13194139534172 false] [17592186128845 1145 "This is a rational function, so it is continuous at all points in its domain. To find its domain, we start by factoring the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= \\frac{x - 3}{x^{2} - 5x + 6} \\\\\n&= (x - 3)/((x - 2)(x - 3)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{x - 2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx \\neq 3\n$$\n\n.\n\nThis function is continuous everywhere except at the excluded points $x=2$ and $x=3$.\n\nTherefore, the domain consists of the union of the intervals $(-\\infty, 2)$, $(2,3)$, and $(3, \\infty)$, namely\n\n$$\n(-\\infty, 2)\\cup(2,3)\\cup(3, \\infty)\n$$\n\nand the function is continuous on these intervals." 13194139534172 false] [17592186128845 1145 "This is a rational function, so it is continuous at all points in its domain. To find its domain, we start by factoring the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x-3}{{x}^{2}-5x+6} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-3}{(x-2)(x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x-2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x \\neq 3$.\n\nThis function is continuous everywhere except at the excluded points $x=2$ and $x=3.$\n\nTherefore, the domain consists of the union of the intervals $(-\\infty, 2),$ $(2,3),$ and $(3, \\infty),$ namely\n\n$$\n(-\\infty, 2)\\cup(2,3)\\cup(3, \\infty)\n$$\n\nand the function is continuous on these intervals." 13194139534172 true] [17592186128846 1100 "Find the largest interval of continuity for the function $f(x)=\\log (2x-3) + x^2+1$." 13194139534172 false] [17592186128846 1100 "Find the largest interval of continuity for the function $f(x)=\\log (2x-3) + x^2+1.$" 13194139534172 true] [17592186128846 1145 "The function consists of two parts, a logarithm, and a polynomial.\n\nThe polynomial $x^2+1$ is continuous at all points in its domain, and its domain consists of all real numbers. So, we focus on the logarithm.\n\nLikewise, the logarithmic function $\\log(2x-3)$ is continuous at all points in its domain. However, the logarithm is only defined for positive inputs, so to find its domain, we set the expression inside the logarithm to be greater than zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-3 & >0 \\\\ 2x & >3 \\\\ x & >\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)=\\log (2x-3) + x^2 + 1$ is continuous on the interval $\\left(\\dfrac 32,\\infty\\right).$" 13194139534172 true] [17592186128846 1145 "The function consists of two parts, a logarithm, and a polynomial.\n\nThe polynomial $x^2+1$ is continuous at all points in its domain, and its domain consists of all real numbers. So, we focus on the logarithm.\n\nLikewise, the logarithmic function $\\log(2x-3)$ is continuous at all points in its domain. However, the logarithm is only defined for positive inputs, so to find its domain, we set the expression inside the logarithm to be greater than zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2x - 3 & > 0 \\\\ 2x & > 3 \\\\ x & > \\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\log (2x-3) + x^2 + 1\n$$\n\nis continuous on the interval\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac 32,\\infty\\right)\n$$" 13194139534172 false] [17592186128847 1100 "The interval $I$ consists of all real numbers $x$ such that $0 \\leq x \\leq 2\\pi$. What is the largest subset of $I$ on which the function $g(x) = \\sin{x}+\\cot{x}$ is continuous?" 13194139534172 false] [17592186128847 1100 "The interval $I$ consists of all real numbers $x$ such that $0 \\leq x \\leq 2\\pi.$ What is the largest subset of $I$ on which the function $g(x) = \\sin{x}+\\cot{x}$ is continuous?" 13194139534172 true] [17592186128847 1145 "Trigonometric functions are continuous at all points in their respective domains.\n\nThe function $\\sin{x}$ is continuous over the entire interval $[0,2\\pi]$, but the function $\\cot{x}$ is not defined at\n\n$$\nx=0, \\pi, 2\\pi\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $g(x)$ consists of the union of the intervals $(0, \\pi)$ and $(\\pi, 2\\pi)$, namely\n\n$$\n(0, \\pi)\\cup(\\pi, 2\\pi)\n$$\n\nand the function is continuous on this union of intervals." 13194139534172 false] [17592186128847 1145 "Trigonometric functions are continuous at all points in their respective domains.\n\nThe function $\\sin{x}$ is continuous over the entire interval $[0,2\\pi],$ but the function $\\cot{x}$ is not defined at $x=0, \\pi, 2\\pi.$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $g(x)$ consists of the union of the intervals $(0, \\pi)$ and $(\\pi, 2\\pi),$ namely\n\n$$\n(0, \\pi)\\cup(\\pi, 2\\pi)\n$$\n\nand the function is continuous on this union of intervals." 13194139534172 true] [17592186129028 1020 #edb/ref 17592186280265 13194139534172 true] [17592186129028 1020 #edb/ref 17592186280272 13194139534172 true] [17592186129028 1020 #edb/ref 17592186280291 13194139534172 true] [17592186129028 1020 #edb/ref 17592186280349 13194139534172 true] [17592186129028 1020 #edb/ref 17592186280356 13194139534172 true] [17592186129029 1020 #edb/ref 17592186280279 13194139534172 true] [17592186129029 1020 #edb/ref 17592186280296 13194139534172 true] [17592186129029 1020 #edb/ref 17592186280314 13194139534172 true] [17592186129029 1020 #edb/ref 17592186280342 13194139534172 true] [17592186129030 1020 #edb/ref 17592186280303 13194139534172 true] [17592186129030 1020 #edb/ref 17592186280308 13194139534172 true] [17592186129030 1020 #edb/ref 17592186280321 13194139534172 true] [17592186129030 1020 #edb/ref 17592186280363 13194139534172 true] [17592186129030 1020 #edb/ref 17592186280370 13194139534172 true] [17592186129031 1020 #edb/ref 17592186280286 13194139534172 true] [17592186129031 1020 #edb/ref 17592186280328 13194139534172 true] [17592186129031 1020 #edb/ref 17592186280335 13194139534172 true] [17592186129692 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534172 true] [17592186129692 1100 "Find the largest union of intervals on which the function $f(x) = \\sin (\\frac{3}{x^{2} - 1})$ is continuous." 13194139534172 false] [17592186129692 1100 "Find the largest union of intervals on which the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}-1})$ is continuous." 13194139534172 true] [17592186129692 1145 "The function is a composition of two functions, a rational function, and a sine function.\n\nThe sine is continuous at all points in its domain, and its domain consists of all real numbers. So, we focus on the rational function.\n\nLikewise, the rational function $\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}-1}$ is continuous at all points in its domain. To find its domain, we start by factoring the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{(x-1)(x+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis function is continuous everywhere except at the excluded points $x=-1$ and $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the domain our original function consists of the union of the intervals $(-\\infty ,-1),$ $(-1,1),$ and $(1,\\infty ),$ namely\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (-1,1)\\cup (1,\\infty )\n$$\n\nand the function is also continuous on these intervals." 13194139534172 true] [17592186129694 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534172 true] [17592186129694 1100 "The interval $I$ consists of all real numbers $x$ such that $-2\\pi \\le x\\le 0.$ What is the largest subset of $I$ on which the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)$ is continuous?" 13194139534172 true] [17592186129694 1100 "The interval $I$ consists of all real numbers $x$ such that $-2π \\le x \\le 0$. What is the largest subset of $I$ on which the function $f(x) = \\cot (x) + \\cos (x)$ is continuous?" 13194139534172 false] [17592186129694 1145 "Trigonometric functions are continuous at all points in their respective domains.\n\nThe function $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is continuous over the entire interval $[-2\\pi ,0],$ but the function $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ is not defined at $x=-2\\pi ,-\\pi ,0.$ \n\nTherefore, the domain of $f(x)$ consists of the union of the intervals $(-2\\pi ,-\\pi )$ and $(-\\pi ,0),$ namely\n\n$$\n(-2\\pi ,-\\pi )\\cup (-\\pi ,0)\n$$\n\nand the function is also continuous on this union of intervals." 13194139534172 true] [17592186129746 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534172 true] [17592186129746 1100 "Determine the largest union of intervals on which the function $f(x) = \\frac{x - 1}{x^{2} - 4x + 3}$ is continuous." 13194139534172 false] [17592186129746 1100 "Determine the largest union of intervals on which the function $f(x)=\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}-4x+3}$ is continuous." 13194139534172 true] [17592186129746 1145 "This is a rational function, so it is continuous at all points in its domain. To find its domain, we start by factoring the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}-4x+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-1}{(x-1)(x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x-3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $x\\ne 1.$\n\nThis function is continuous everywhere except at the excluded points $x=1$ and $x=3.$\n\nTherefore, the domain consists of the union of the intervals $(-\\infty ,1),$ $(1,3),$ and $(3,\\infty ),$ namely\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,1)\\cup (1,3)\\cup (3,\\infty )\n$$\n\nand the function is also continuous on this union of intervals." 13194139534172 true] [17592186280265 1017 #edb/ref 17592186280266 13194139534172 true] [17592186280265 1089 "q-105435" 13194139534172 true] [17592186280265 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534172 true] [17592186280265 1100 "Determine the largest interval on which the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{12-4x}+\\sqrt{5x+8}$ is continuous." 13194139534172 true] [17592186280265 1113 #uuid "7ed2a43e-3f1c-549e-b29f-a03fa40bc58f" 13194139534172 true] [17592186280265 1145 "Our function consists of two radical functions, so it is continuous over all points in its domain.\n\n- To find the domain of $\\sqrt{12-4x}$, we set the expression under the radical to be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12-4x\\ge 0\\,⟹\\,4x\\le 12\\,⟹\\,x\\le 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To find the domain of $\\sqrt{5x+8}$, we set the expression under the radical to be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+8\\ge 0\\,⟹\\,5x\\ge -8\\,⟹\\,x\\ge -\\frac{8}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe domain of $f(x)$ is given by the intersection of the domain of our radical functions:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,3]\\cap [-\\frac{8}{5},\\infty )=[-\\frac{8}{5},3],\n$$\n\nand the function is continuous on this interval." 13194139534172 true] [17592186280266 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280267 13194139534172 true] [17592186280266 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280268 13194139534172 true] [17592186280266 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280269 13194139534172 true] [17592186280266 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280270 13194139534172 true] [17592186280266 1000 #edb/ref 17592186280271 13194139534172 true] [17592186280266 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534172 true] [17592186280266 1041 "selection" 13194139534172 true] [17592186280266 1104 #edb/ref 17592186280269 13194139534172 true] [17592186280266 1110 #uuid "17473335-7adf-53f2-8e82-88d8d5a24a45" 13194139534172 true] [17592186280267 1034 "(-\\infty ,3]" 13194139534172 true] [17592186280267 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 131